WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.120
Hi, I'm Hap.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.040
Today we're looking at why npm
matters so much in web development.

3
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.360
The name covers three separate things,

4
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.840
and every one of them is
doing real work for you.

5
00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:19.413
npm is a program on your computer.

6
00:00:19.653 --> 00:00:24.053
npm is also a library on the internet,
full of code other people wrote.

7
00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.620
And npm is a format, an
agreement about what

8
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.660
a downloadable folder of
JavaScript looks like.

9
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.100
One name, three jobs, and between them
they're how a website gets built today.

10
00:00:37.906 --> 00:00:40.386
Let's start with the one on your computer.

11
00:00:40.546 --> 00:00:44.706
When you install Node, npm
is automatically included,

12
00:00:44.706 --> 00:00:47.666
even though they are
completely separate programs.

13
00:00:48.146 --> 00:00:50.711
Checking their versions
returns two different

14
00:00:50.711 --> 00:00:54.231
numbers because they handle
entirely different tasks.

15
00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:57.271
Node executes your JavaScript code,

16
00:00:57.271 --> 00:01:01.591
while npm downloads the external
packages and libraries you need.

17
00:01:03.373 --> 00:01:07.533
The npm registry is an online
library containing millions

18
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:11.533
of packages that you can download
without ever creating an account.

19
00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:14.093
Along with a description of the package,

20
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.810
there is a direct link to
its source code repository.

21
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.490
This link is incredibly
valuable because it allows you

22
00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.490
to thoroughly inspect and read the code
before downloading it to your system.

23
00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:30.255
The package on this page is Eleventy,

24
00:01:30.255 --> 00:01:35.935
a static site generator that
can turn a folder of templates
into a multipage website.

25
00:01:36.255 --> 00:01:39.615
That is the kind of tool
npm exists to deliver,

26
00:01:39.615 --> 00:01:43.160
because somebody else built
it, published it under a name,

27
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.520
and your project installs it
instead of rebuilding it.

28
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.360
Almost every website you work on is
assembled from tools like this one.

29
00:01:52.171 --> 00:01:55.851
The third meaning of npm
refers to its package format.

30
00:01:55.851 --> 00:02:01.451
A package is simply a folder of
JavaScript code published with a
specific name and version number,

31
00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:04.178
which together act as a unique identifier.

32
00:02:04.178 --> 00:02:06.578
When you request a package,
the name specifies

33
00:02:06.578 --> 00:02:10.178
which package and the version
determines the exact release,

34
00:02:10.178 --> 00:02:13.058
ensuring you receive reproducible
code for your project.

35
00:02:14.928 --> 00:02:18.208
Now watch one command
use all three at once.

36
00:02:18.448 --> 00:02:19.568
You type

37
00:02:19.568 --> 00:02:21.728
npm install and a package name.

38
00:02:21.888 --> 00:02:27.248
The program on your computer
reads that name, asks the
library on the internet for it,

39
00:02:27.443 --> 00:02:31.843
and unpacks what comes back into
a folder called node_modules.

40
00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:38.323
The contents of that folder are fully
operational programs and plain,

41
00:02:38.323 --> 00:02:41.523
readable JavaScript libraries
that build and run your website.

42
00:02:42.053 --> 00:02:46.533
Because none of the code is hidden or
compiled into an unreadable format,

43
00:02:46.613 --> 00:02:50.053
you can open, inspect, and
read any file at any time.

44
00:02:52.018 --> 00:02:55.458
For many projects that
node_modules folder gets big.

45
00:02:55.458 --> 00:02:59.538
And, while your project needs
it, the folder should never
go into your repository,

46
00:02:59.538 --> 00:03:02.258
because you can always
get it again when needed.

47
00:03:02.258 --> 00:03:06.803
Every last piece of it came from that
library and can come back the same way.

48
00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:13.283
There are many reasons why a file or
folder should never be pushed to GitHub,

49
00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:16.723
but almost always the list
includes node_modules.

50
00:03:17.258 --> 00:03:21.578
To not push certain files,
you tell git to ignore them

51
00:03:21.578 --> 00:03:26.618
by always having a .gitignore file
in the repository's root folder.

52
00:03:28.273 --> 00:03:32.193
This file, with its very
specific name and location,

53
00:03:32.593 --> 00:03:35.473
lists what files should
never be pushed to GitHub.

54
00:03:35.984 --> 00:03:37.424
So that's npm.

55
00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:41.504
A program, a library, and a
format, all under one name.

56
00:03:41.664 --> 00:03:45.024
Every project you build from here
on is going to use all three.
